{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 59, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "זאת תורת נגע", :translit "zot torat nega", :gloss "this is the law of the plague"} {:he "תורת המצורע", :translit "torat ha-metzora", :gloss "the law of the metzora"} {:he "תורת אשר בו", :translit "torat asher bo", :gloss "the law of that in which it is"} {:he "זאת התורה", :translit "zot ha-torah", :gloss "this is the law"} {:he "תורת הצרעת", :translit "torat ha-tzara'at", :gloss "the law of tzara'at"} {:he "ה' חומשים", :translit "chamesh chomeshim", :gloss "five fifths"} {:he "ה' חומשי תורה", :translit "chamesh chomshei torah", :gloss "five fifths of the Torah"} {:he "י' פרשיות", :translit "eser parshiyot", :gloss "ten sections"} {:he "נגעי אדם", :translit "neg'ei adam", :gloss "plagues of people"} {:he "נגעי בגדים", :translit "neg'ei begadim", :gloss "plagues of garments"} {:he "נגעי בתים", :translit "neg'ei batim", :gloss "plagues of houses"} {:he "י' הדברות", :translit "eser ha-dibrot", :gloss "ten commandments"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Five phrases beginning with תורת/זאת corresponding to the five books (חומשים) of the Torah"} {:kind :other, :note "Ten plague-sections (people, garments, houses) set against the Ten Commandments as a corresponding remedy"}], :frame {:text "The premise that neglecting any portion of Torah violates the whole appears in the idiom of 'five fifths' (חומשי תורה), and the broader principle that violating one command is as transgressing all is expressed at Shabbat 70a and Horayot 8a. The specific correlation of five 'torah' phrases with the five books of the Pentateuch is a structural reading of Leviticus 13:59–14:54, where the phrase 'זאת תורת' recurs for each type of צרעת. The association of צרעת with ethical or covenantal failure draws on the characterization of לשון הרע as a cause of צרעת at Arakhin 15b–16a.", :sources ["Shabbat 70a" "Horayot 8a" "Leviticus 13:59" "Leviticus 14:2" "Leviticus 14:32" "Leviticus 14:54" "Arakhin 15b"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Five phrases beginning with תורת/זאת corresponding to the five books (חומשים) of the Torah", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Ten plague-sections (people, garments, houses) set against the Ten Commandments as a corresponding remedy", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:23:24.602306173Z", :model "glm-5.2"}